1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,811
I am James Rosing.

2
00:00:01,811 --> 00:00:10,415
I am a surgeon in Newport Beach, and I
have been getting letters like this one
for fourteen years.

3
00:00:10,415 --> 00:00:15,849
“Not medically necessary.” A payer’s
software writes that in about a second.

4
00:00:15,849 --> 00:00:18,566
Answering it takes a physician hours.

5
00:00:18,566 --> 00:00:24,000
So most denials are never appealed, and
the patient absorbs the cost.

6
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:30,207
People say AI nets to zero here, because
both sides can buy the same model.

7
00:00:30,207 --> 00:00:35,586
A denial is a claim about a policy; the
payer holds the policy.

8
00:00:35,586 --> 00:00:41,379
An appeal is a claim about the record;
only the physician holds the record.

9
00:00:41,379 --> 00:00:50,897
This project writes the appeal: a draft
the physician reads and signs, every
sentence checked by a gate that is not a
model.

10
00:00:50,897 --> 00:01:00,000
One of fifty synthetic cases, no real
patient anywhere: a 47-year-old woman,
radiation planning after lumpectomy,
denied by her Medicare Advantage plan.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,452
Watch the model.

12
00:01:01,452 --> 00:01:11,129
Meta Spark 1.3 asks my coverage index
when this plan’s rules last changed,
then opens only the entries it needs.

13
00:01:11,129 --> 00:01:15,000
Each sentence quotes one entry and names
it.

14
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:25,000
As each sentence arrives, a
deterministic gate checks that the quote
is really in that entry and that every
number, date, and code matches the
source.

15
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:26,154
Pass or refuse.

16
00:01:26,154 --> 00:01:30,000
Eight of eight verified, in twelve
seconds, for two cents.

17
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:34,390
Change one number in a verified sentence
and the gate refuses it.

18
00:01:34,390 --> 00:01:36,585
The model drafts; the record decides.

19
00:01:36,585 --> 00:01:45,000
The payer can run the same gate on my
appeal: the dispute is settled on the
record, not on whose bot drafted better.

20
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,280
When the chart cannot support an
argument, the model says so:

21
00:01:50,280 --> 00:01:57,000
Muse Wonder read all twenty-two entries
for a sepsis criterion, found nothing,
and declined.

22
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:01,500
Fifty cases, three Meta models,
including the open-weight one, nine
runs.

23
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:05,182
Agreement with the key, forty-five to
fifty of fifty.

24
00:02:05,182 --> 00:02:07,636
No fabricated quote in any sentence.

25
00:02:07,636 --> 00:02:09,682
Under a dollar a run.

26
00:02:09,682 --> 00:02:15,000
Ninety-five hundred fabrications
injected to attack the gate: every hard
one was caught.

27
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:26,250
This is what the physician sees: the
appeal, the payer’s reason answered
directly, every sentence footnoted to
the entry it quotes.

28
00:02:26,250 --> 00:02:30,000
A draft until a human signs it.

29
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,784
The gate is on npm as litmus-gate, for
either side of the table.

30
00:02:38,784 --> 00:02:44,189
The code and the fifty cases are public.

31
00:02:44,189 --> 00:02:53,649
Next, my own denials, then prior
authorizations, the same problem in the
other direction.

32
00:02:53,649 --> 00:02:55,000
Thank you.
